// Written from the CIA World Factbook (final 2026 edition, public domain).
export const FUN_FACTS = {
  fr: ["France comes from the Latin Francia, 'Land of the Franks', a group of Germanic tribes whose name may come from an Old German word meaning 'brave'.","France's national symbols include the Gallic rooster, the fleur-de-lis and Marianne, a woman who personifies the country.","France's anthem, 'La Marseillaise', got its name when the National Guard of Marseille sang it while marching into Paris in 1792."],
  gb: ["The name Britain, as in the United Kingdom's Great Britain, probably comes from the Celtic word 'pretani', meaning 'painted people'.","Thanks to its heavily indented coastline, no place in the United Kingdom is more than 125 km from tidal waters.","In the United Kingdom, Scotland's national symbols include a unicorn and a thistle, while Wales has a dragon, a daffodil and a leek."],
  ie: ["Dublin, Ireland's capital, comes from the Irish words 'dubh' (black or dark) and 'linn' (pool), referring to the color of the Liffey River.","Ireland's Irish name, Eire, may come from the Old Celtic 'iveriu', meaning 'good land'.","Ireland has two national symbols: the harp and the shamrock, also known as a trefoil."],
  be: ["Brussels, Belgium's capital, has a Germanic name built from 'broca' (marsh) and 'sali' (room or building).","Belgium sits at the crossroads of Western Europe: most West European capitals are within 1,000 km of Brussels.","Legend says the lyrics of Belgium's anthem, 'La Brabanconne', were written by an actor and a group of young people in a Brussels cafe."],
  nl: ["The Netherlands means 'the lowlands', and about a quarter of the country lies below sea level.","Amsterdam, in the Netherlands, is named after the Amstel River plus the Dutch word 'dam'; Amstel may come from Germanic words for 'current' and 'place'.","The Netherlands' highest point, Mount Scenery at 862 m, is on the island of Saba in the Caribbean."],
  lu: ["Luxembourg's name probably comes from Lucilinburhuc, thought to mean 'little fortress'; it first named the city, then the whole country.","Luxembourg has a royal anthem, 'De Wilhelmus', played when members of the grand ducal family enter or exit a ceremony.","Luxembourg was founded way back in 963 and became a grand duchy in 1815."],
  mc: ["Monaco is the second-smallest independent state in the world, after the Holy See, and the smallest country with a coastline.","Monaco's name might come from the Greek 'monoikos' (solitary), the Ligurian 'monegu' (rock) or the Basque 'muno' (mountain).","Monaco's national symbol is a pattern of red and white lozenges, or diamond shapes."],
  es: ["One theory says Spain's name, España, comes from the Punic word 'span', meaning 'rabbit'.","Spain's national anthem has no lyrics, and it was the first national anthem to be officially adopted.","Spain's highest point isn't on the mainland: it's Pico de Teide on Tenerife in the Canary Islands, at 3,718 m."],
  pt: ["Portugal comes from the Roman 'Portus Cale'; Cale may come from the Latin 'calere' (to be warm) because its harbor never iced over.","Portugal's national symbol is the armillary sphere, a spherical astrolabe for modeling objects in the sky.","Lisbon, Portugal's capital, may get its name from the Phoenician 'alis-ubbo', meaning 'joyful bay', or from the Greek hero Ulysses."],
  it: ["Italy's name may come from the Vitali tribe, whose name is believed to be linked to the Latin word 'vitulus', meaning 'calf'.","Rome, Italy's capital, is traditionally named after its legendary founder Romulus, but the name may come from an Etruscan name for the Tiber River.","Italy's national symbol is a five-pointed white star called the Stella d'Italia."],
  ad: ["Andorra's national symbol is a red cow.","Andorra sits high in the Pyrenees: its mean elevation is 1,996 m, and even its lowest point, Riu Runer, is at 840 m.","Andorra's anthem, 'El Gran Carlemany' (The Great Charlemagne), tells a brief history of the country in the first person."],
  sm: ["San Marino claims to be the world's oldest republic; tradition says a stonemason named Marinus founded it in A.D. 301.","San Marino is completely surrounded by Italy and is dominated by the Apennine Mountains.","San Marino's anthem has no lyrics, and its music is based on a 10th-century chorale piece."],
  va: ["Vatican City is the world's smallest state, an enclave inside Rome, Italy.","Vatican City is named for Vatican Hill, from the Latin 'vates' (prophet), after the fortune tellers who frequented the area in Roman times.","Vatican City's highest point is the Vatican Gardens at 78 m, and its lowest is Saint Peter's Square at 19 m."],
  mt: ["The ancient Greeks called Malta 'Melite', possibly from 'meli', meaning 'honey', after the island's honey production.","Malta boasts some of the oldest megalithic sites in the world.","Malta is an archipelago, but only its three largest islands, Malta, Gozo and Comino, are inhabited."],
  de: ["Germany's native name comes from 'Deutsch', from the Old High German 'diutisc', meaning 'national'.","Berlin, Germany's capital, may come from the Old Slavic word 'berl' or 'birl', meaning 'swamp', after its original site by the Spree River.","Germany's Black Forest is the source of the Danube, a 2,888 km river that flows east through many countries."],
  ch: ["Switzerland is named after Schwyz, one of the founding cantons of the Swiss Confederacy in the late 13th century.","Switzerland's anthem, the Swiss Psalm, has four official versions: German, French, Italian and Romansh.","Bern, Switzerland's capital, is sometimes linked to the German 'Baer' (bear), though 'marshy place' is a more likely origin."],
  at: ["Austria is home to the Eisriesenwelt, the world's largest and longest ice cave system at 42 km, inside the Hochkogel mountain.","Austria's German name, Oesterreich, means 'eastern realm' and dates back to the 10th century.","Austria's national symbols include the eagle, the edelweiss and the Alpine gentian."],
  li: ["Liechtenstein and Uzbekistan are the only two doubly landlocked countries in the world.","Liechtenstein's anthem, 'Oben am jungen Rhein', uses the same tune as the United Kingdom's 'God Save the King'.","Liechtenstein is named after the Liechtenstein family, whose name came from an Austrian castle; in German it means 'light stone'."],
  cz: ["Czechia's Hranice Abyss is the world's deepest surveyed freshwater cave at 519 m, and it may be 800 to 1,200 m deep.","Czechia's national symbol is a silver (or white) double-tailed rampant lion.","Prague, Czechia's capital, may come from the old Slavic word 'praga' or 'prah', meaning 'threshold'."],
  pl: ["Poland's name probably comes from the Slavic word 'pole', meaning 'field' or 'plain', after the country's flat land.","Warsaw, Poland's capital, began as a fishing village, and several legends link its founding to a man named Wars or Warsz.","The Vistula, Poland's longest river at 1,213 km, has both its source and its mouth in Poland."],
  sk: ["Slovakia's anthem, 'Storm Over the Tatras', is based on an 1843 Slovak folk song called 'She Was Digging a Well'.","Slovakia's national symbol is a double-barred cross standing over three peaks.","Bratislava, Slovakia's capital, only got that name in 1919, replacing its old name, Prešporok."],
  hu: ["Hungary's name traces back to 'Hungari', which originally meant an 'alliance of ten tribes'.","Hungary's capital was formed in 1873 when Buda and Pest merged; Pest comes from a Slavic word meaning 'furnace' or 'oven'.","Hungary's national symbol is the Holy Crown of Hungary, also called the Crown of Saint Stephen."],
  no: ["Norway's name comes from the Old Norse 'norre' and 'vegr', meaning 'northern way', after its long western coastline.","Norway has some 50,000 islands off its coast and one of the longest, most rugged coastlines in the world, at 25,148 km.","Oslo, Norway's capital, may come from the Old Norwegian word 'os', meaning 'estuary', as the city sits on a fjord."],
  se: ["Sweden has almost 100,000 lakes; the largest, Vanern, is the third-largest lake in Europe.","Sweden's local name, Sverige, means 'kingdom of the Svea', a tribe whose name probably meant 'independent'.","Sweden's national symbols are three crowns and a lion, and its national colors are blue and yellow."],
  fi: ["Finland is named after the Finns, whose name comes from the Germanic word 'finna', meaning 'fish scale'.","Helsinki, Finland's capital, is the northernmost national capital on the European continent.","Finland's anthem, 'Maamme' (Our Land), was never officially adopted but has been popular since a student group first sang it in 1848."],
  dk: ["Copenhagen, Denmark's capital, gets its name from the Danish words 'køber' (merchant or buyer) and 'havn' (harbor or port).","Denmark is made up of the Jutland Peninsula and more than 400 islands.","Denmark's national symbols are the lion and the mute swan."],
  is: ["Reykjavík, Iceland's capital, is the world's northernmost national capital, and its name means 'smoky bay' after steam from hot springs.","Iceland has more land covered by glaciers than all of continental Europe.","Iceland's national symbol is the gyrfalcon."],
  ee: ["Estonia's anthem has the same melody as Finland's anthem, but with different lyrics.","Estonia has over 1,500 islands lying off its coast.","Estonia's national symbols are the barn swallow and the cornflower."],
  lv: ["Latvia's national symbol is a bird: the white wagtail.","Latvia's national colors are maroon and white.","Riga, Latvia's capital, may come from the Old Lithuanian 'ringa' (bend), for its spot on the Western Dvina River, or Latvian 'ridzina' (stream)."],
  lt: ["Lithuania's name may come from local words meaning 'small river' or 'rain', making it possibly the 'land of rain'.","Lithuania's national symbols are a mounted knight called Vytis (the Chaser) and the white stork.","Vilnius, Lithuania's capital, is named after the Vilnia River, from the Lithuanian word 'vilnis', meaning 'wave'."],
  gr: ["Greece has an archipelago of about 2,000 islands and a coastline of 13,676 km.","Greece's anthem, 'Hymn to Freedom', comes from a 158-stanza poem, but only the first two stanzas are used. Cyprus uses it too.","By tradition, Athens, Greece's capital, is named after Athena, goddess of wisdom, though the name is probably pre-Hellenic."],
  hr: ["Croatia has some 1,200 islands, islets, ridges and rocks, and 4,058 km of its 5,835 km coastline belongs to islands.","Croatia's national symbol is a red-and-white checkerboard.","Zagreb, Croatia's capital, means 'beyond the bank (or ditch)', from the Old Croat 'za' (beyond) and 'greb' (bank or ditch)."],
  si: ["Slovenia's national symbol is a mountain: Mount Triglav, which at 2,864 m is also the country's highest point.","Ljubljana, Slovenia's capital, is traditionally linked to the Slovene word 'ljubljena', meaning 'beloved'.","Slovenia's anthem, 'Zdravljica' (A Toast), uses only the seventh verse of a poem written in 1848."],
  rs: ["Belgrade, Serbia's capital, means 'white city', from 'beo' (white) and 'grad' (city), probably after the white stone of its fortress.","Serbia's national symbol is a white double-headed eagle.","Serbia's anthem, 'Boze pravde' (God of Justice), was originally written as part of a play in 1872."],
  ba: ["Bosnia and Herzegovina's anthem has no officially adopted lyrics, so it remains officially wordless.","Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina's capital, gets its name from the Turkish word 'saray', meaning 'palace' or 'mansion'.","In Bosnia and Herzegovina, Bosnia is named for the Bosna River, while Herzegovina means 'dukedom', from the Old Serbian 'herceg' (duke)."],
  me: ["Montenegro means 'dark mountain' in Italian, after the dark coniferous forests in its mountains.","Podgorica, Montenegro's capital, means 'under the mountain', from 'pod' (under) and 'gora' (mountain).","Montenegro shares Lake Scutari, the largest lake in the Balkans, with Albania."],
  al: ["Albania's local name, Shqiperia, comes from the word for 'eagle' and is popularly read as 'Land of the Eagles'.","Albania's national symbol is a black double-headed eagle, and its national colors are red and black.","Albania shares Lake Scutari, the largest lake in the Balkans, with Montenegro."],
  mk: ["North Macedonia's national symbol is an eight-rayed sun.","North Macedonia's name comes from the ancient kingdom of Macedon, possibly from a Greek word for 'tail' or an Illyrian word for 'cattle'.","Skopje, North Macedonia's capital, gets its name from Scupi, what it was called in Roman times."],
  xk: ["In Kosovo, the 41 km Nerodimka River splits in two: one branch ends up in the Black Sea and the other in the Aegean Sea.","Kosovo's name may come from the Serbian word 'kos', meaning 'blackbird', or from a personal name.","Kosovo's anthem is called 'Europe', and it has no lyrics."],
  ro: ["Romania's name comes from the Latin 'Romani', meaning 'people from Rome'.","Bucharest, Romania's capital, is said to be named after a shepherd called Bucur, who is reputed to have founded the town in 1457.","The Danube, 2,888 km long, reaches its mouth in Romania."],
  md: ["Moldova's national symbol is the aurochs, a type of wild cattle.","Moldova is named after the Moldova River in Romania, whose name probably comes from a root word meaning 'dark' or 'black'.","Chișinău, Moldova's capital, may get its name from the Old Moldovan word 'kishineu', meaning 'spring' or 'artesian well'."],
  bg: ["Sofia, Bulgaria's capital, is named after the Church of Saint Sofia, parts of which may date to the 4th century.","Bulgaria is named after the Bulgar tribes, whose name may come from the Turkic word 'bulga', meaning 'mixed'.","Musala, at 2,925 m, is Bulgaria's highest point, and the country also has 354 km of Black Sea coastline."],
  ua: ["Ukraine's national symbols are the tryzub (trident) and the sunflower.","Ukraine is the second-largest country in Europe, after Russia.","Kyiv, Ukraine's capital, is traditionally named after Prince Kiy, who is said to have founded the city in the 9th century."],
  by: ["Belarus has 11,000 lakes, thanks to glacial scouring, which also explains why its land is so flat.","Belarus means 'White Rus', from the Slavic word 'bel' (white) and 'Rus', an Old East Slavic ethnic name.","Belarus has no official national symbol, but its traditional one is a mounted knight called Pahonia (the Chaser)."],
  ru: ["Russia's Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world and is estimated to hold one fifth of the world's fresh surface water.","Russia's Mount Elbrus, at 5,642 m, is the highest point in Europe.","Russia is the largest country in the world by area, and its national symbols include the bear and the double-headed eagle."],
  co: ["Colombia is named after the explorer Christopher Columbus.","Colombia is the only South American country with coastlines on both the North Pacific Ocean and the Caribbean Sea.","Colombia's capital was first named Santa Fe de Bacatá in 1538, after a nearby Chibcha settlement; over time the name was corrupted into Bogotá."],
  ve: ["Venezuela means 'Little Venice': in 1499, stilt houses on Lake Maracaibo reminded explorers of the buildings of Venice, Italy.","Venezuela is home to Angel Falls, the world's highest waterfall at 979 m, which plunges from a table-top mountain called Auyan Tepui.","Venezuela's capital, Caracas, is named after the Caracas tribe who first settled the area, but what their name meant is unknown."],
  ec: ["Ecuador's name is simply the Spanish word for 'equator', after where the country sits on the globe.","Ecuador's capital, Quito, is named after the Quitu, a pre-Columbian people who lived there; nobody knows what their name meant.","When Ecuador's national anthem is performed, only the chorus and the second verse are sung."],
  pe: ["The name of Peru's capital, Lima, comes from the Quechua name Rimak, from rima ('to speak'); priests once spoke to worshippers from inside statues of their gods.","The Amazon River begins in Peru: its ultimate source is a remote slope of Nevado Mismi, a 5,316 m peak.","Peru's national symbol is the vicuna, a camelid related to the llama."],
  gy: ["Guyana's name comes from Guiana, which may come from a local term meaning 'Land of Water', for the area's many rivers and streams.","Guyana is the only English-speaking country in South America.","Guyana's national symbols include the jaguar, the Victoria Regia water lily and the Canje pheasant, also called the hoatzin."],
  sr: ["Suriname is the smallest independent country on the South American continent.","Suriname's national anthem has lyrics in both Dutch and Sranang Tongo, and it began as a Sunday-school song written in 1893.","Suriname's capital, Paramaribo, gets its name from the Guaraní words para (water or river) and maribo (inhabitants)."],
  br: ["Brazil is named after the brazil tree, which once grew plentifully along its coast and was used to make a deep red dye.","Brazil's national symbol is in the sky: the Southern Cross constellation.","Most of the Pantanal, the world's largest tropical wetland, lies in west central Brazil."],
  ar: ["Argentina's name comes from a Spanish word for 'silver', possibly describing the land by the Rio de la Plata, the 'Silver River'.","Argentina's capital, Buenos Aires, means 'fair winds' in Spanish.","Argentina has both the Western Hemisphere's tallest mountain, Cerro Aconcagua (6,962 m), and its lowest point, Laguna del Carbón (-105 m)."],
  cl: ["Chile is the longest country north-to-south in the world, stretching across 39 degrees of latitude.","In Chile's Atacama Desert, the driest in the world, the Ojos del Salado volcano holds the world's highest lake, at 6,390 m.","Nobody is sure where Chile's name comes from; it may be a local word meaning 'land's end' or 'cold'."],
  uy: ["Uruguay's national anthem has the world's longest music of any anthem: 105 bars, almost five minutes, and usually only the first verse and chorus are sung.","Uruguay is named after the Uruguay River, whose name comes from the Guarani words uru (bird) and guay (tail).","One theory says the name of Uruguay's capital, Montevideo, combines the Spanish monte (mountain) with the Latin video (I see)."],
  py: ["Paraguay is named after its river, whose name may come from the Guarani words para (water or river) and guay (born).","Paraguay's capital, Asunción, means 'assumption' in Spanish: it was founded on August 15, 1537, the feast day of the Assumption.","In Paraguay, both Spanish and Guarani are official languages, and about a third of people speak only Guarani."],
  bo: ["Bolivia is named in honor of Simón Bolívar, a 19th-century leader of South American independence.","Bolivia shares Lake Titicaca with Peru; at 3,805 m, it is the world's highest navigable lake.","Bolivia has two national flowers, the cantuta and the patuju, and the llama is one of its national symbols."],
  us: ["The 'America' in United States of America comes from the first name of Italian explorer Amerigo Vespucci; the name was first used in 1507.","Bracken Cave in Texas, United States, is the world's largest bat cave: an estimated 20 million Mexican free-tailed bats roost there from March to October.","The United States anthem, 'The Star-Spangled Banner', was set to the tune of 'The Anacreontic Song', and only the first of its four verses is sung."],
  ca: ["Canada's name probably comes from the Huron or Iroquois word kanata, meaning 'village' or 'camp'.","Canada has at least 2 million and possibly over 3 million lakes, more than all other countries combined.","Canada's capital, Ottawa, is named after the Ottawa River, whose name comes from the Algonquin word adawe, meaning 'to trade'."],
  mx: ["Mexico's name may come from Metztlixihtlico, an Aztec name for its capital that probably meant 'the center of the moon'.","Near the town of Chicxulub, on Mexico's Yucatán coast, lie the remnants of a huge asteroid or comet crater about 150 km across, linked to the end of the dinosaurs.","Mexico's national symbols are the golden eagle and the dahlia flower."],
  gt: ["Guatemala's name comes from Quauhtemallan, a Nahuatl name meaning 'land of the eagle', which the Spanish probably pronounced 'Guatemala'.","Volcan Tajumulco in Guatemala, at 4,220 m, is the highest point in Central America.","The words of Guatemala's anthem were sent in anonymously to a contest by Cuban poet Jose Joaquin Palma, and his authorship stayed secret until 1911."],
  bz: ["Belize's name may come from the Spanish pronunciation of Wallace, a Scottish explorer who settled there in 1638, or from a Maya word meaning 'muddy-watered'.","Belize's national symbols include Baird's tapir, a large forest-dwelling mammal, the keel-billed toucan and the black orchid.","Belize is the only country in Central America without a coastline on the North Pacific Ocean."],
  hn: ["Honduras means 'depths' in Spanish, after the deep anchorage in the northern Bay of Trujillo.","The capital of Honduras, Tegucigalpa, is a Nahuatl word meaning 'silver mountain', probably after nearby silver mines.","The scarlet macaw and the white-tailed deer are national symbols of Honduras."],
  sv: ["El Salvador is the smallest country in Central America and the only one without a coastline on the Caribbean Sea.","El Salvador's national anthem lasts four minutes and 20 seconds, making it one of the longest in the world.","El Salvador's national symbol is a bird called the turquoise-browed motmot."],
  ni: ["Nicaragua's name is said to combine Nicarao, the name of a local chieftain, with the Spanish agua (water), for the country's two large lakes.","Nicaragua is the largest country in Central America and holds its largest freshwater body, Lago de Nicaragua, at 8,150 sq km.","Nicaragua's capital, Managua, is named after Lake Managua, from the Guaraní words ama (rain) and nagua (spirit), referring to a local deity."],
  cr: ["Costa Rica means 'rich coast' in Spanish; Christopher Columbus named it in 1502 for the region's abundant vegetation and water.","Four volcanoes, two of them active, rise near San José in the center of Costa Rica.","Costa Rica's anthem music was first written for an 1852 welcome ceremony for US and UK diplomats; the words were only added in 1900."],
  pa: ["Panama's name has an unclear origin, but it may come from a Guarani word meaning 'place of many fish'.","Panama controls the Panama Canal, which links the North Atlantic Ocean with the North Pacific Ocean via the Caribbean Sea.","Panama's national symbol is the harpy eagle."],
  cu: ["Cuba is the largest country in the Caribbean and the westernmost island of the Greater Antilles.","Cuba's name may come from a Taino word: either cubao, 'where fertile land is abundant', or coabana, 'great place'.","Cuba's capital was named San Cristobal de la Habana; 'Habana' may have been the name of a local group, but its meaning is unknown."],
  jm: ["Jamaica's name comes from the Arawak word xaymaca, meaning 'Land of Wood and Water' or possibly 'Land of Springs'.","Jamaica is the third-largest island in the Caribbean, after Cuba and Hispaniola.","Jamaica's national symbols include a bird, the green-and-black streamertail."],
  ht: ["Haiti comes from the Arawak name Ayti, 'Land of Mountains', and fittingly it is the most mountainous nation in the Caribbean.","Haiti's capital, Port-au-Prince, means 'the port of the prince', probably after a ship called The Prince that anchored in the bay in the early 1700s.","Haiti's national symbols are a bird, the Hispaniolan trogon, and the hibiscus flower."],
  do: ["The Dominican Republic's name comes from Santo Domingo, meaning 'holy Sunday': Spanish explorers first settled the island on a Sunday in 1496.","The Dominican Republic has the Caribbean's tallest mountain, Pico Duarte (3,098 m), and its lowest point and largest lake, Lago Enriquillo (-46 m).","The Dominican Republic's national symbol is a bird called the palmchat."],
  bs: ["The name Bahamas may come from the Spanish baja mar, meaning 'low sea', describing the shallow waters of the Bahama Banks.","The Bahamas' national symbols include the blue marlin, the flamingo and the yellow elderflower, and one of its national colors is aquamarine.","The highest point in the Bahamas is only 64 m above sea level, on Cat Island."],
  kn: ["Saint Kitts and Nevis is the smallest country in the Western Hemisphere, in both area and population.","Columbus named Saint Kitts, in Saint Kitts and Nevis, after his patron saint, Saint Christopher. Kit was a common nickname for Christopher.","In Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Kitts is shaped like a baseball bat and Nevis like a ball, separated by a 3 km channel called The Narrows."],
  ag: ["Antigua and Barbuda's names mean 'old' and 'bearded' in Spanish; Barbuda's 'beard' may be its lichen-covered fig trees.","The highest point in Antigua and Barbuda is called Mount Obama, at 402 m.","Antigua and Barbuda's national symbol is the fallow deer."],
  dm: ["Dominica was named by Christopher Columbus for the day he spotted it, a Sunday (Domingo in Spanish), on 3 November 1493.","Dominica's Boiling Lake is the second-largest thermally active lake in the world.","Dominica's national symbols are the sisserou parrot and the Carib wood flower."],
  lc: ["Saint Lucia is believed to be named after Saint Lucy by French sailors who were shipwrecked there on 13 December 1502, her feast day.","Saint Lucia's national symbols include the twin Pitons, two striking cone-shaped volcanic peaks, and the Saint Lucia parrot.","Saint Lucia's capital was a village called Carenage until 1785, when it was renamed Castries after a French Minister of the Navy and Colonies."],
  vc: ["Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is named after Saint Vincent of Saragossa: Columbus found the island on 22 January 1498, the saint's feast day.","Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is made up of 32 islands and cays, and its highest point, La Soufriere, stands at a neat 1,234 m.","Saint Vincent and the Grenadines' national symbol is the Saint Vincent parrot."],
  bb: ["Barbados means 'the bearded ones' in Spanish, after either the beard-like leaves of its fig trees or the beards of its Carib inhabitants.","Barbados is the easternmost island in the Caribbean.","Barbados' national symbols include Neptune's trident, the pelican and the red bird of paradise flower, also known as 'Pride of Barbados'."],
  gd: ["Grenada's name may come from the Spanish city of Granada; in Spanish, granada means 'pomegranate'.","Grenada's national symbols are the Grenada dove and the bougainvillea flower.","In the 20th century, nutmeg became Grenada's leading export."],
  tt: ["In Trinidad and Tobago, Trinidad means 'Trinity', possibly for its three mountain peaks, and Tobago may be named for local tobacco or its cigar shape.","Pitch Lake, on the southwest coast of Trinidad in Trinidad and Tobago, is the world's largest natural reservoir of asphalt.","Trinidad and Tobago has a national bird for each island: the scarlet ibis for Trinidad and the cocrico for Tobago."],
  tr: ["Turkey's capital Ankara probably gets its name from the ancient root word ang, meaning 'bend', after its original spot in a winding gorge.","Istanbul in Turkey straddles the Bosporus and is the only metropolis in the world located on two continents.","Turkey's highest point, Mount Ararat at 5,137 m, is reputed to be the landing place of Noah's ark."],
  cy: ["The name Cyprus probably comes from the Sumerian kabar, meaning 'copper' or 'bronze'; the island had copper mines in ancient times.","Cyprus has a wild sheep, the Cypriot mouflon, as one of its national symbols, alongside the white dove.","Cyprus is the third largest island in the Mediterranean Sea, after Sicily and Sardinia."],
  sy: ["The name Syria appears as 'Suri' in Babylonian cuneiform writings dating from about 4000 B.C.","Syria's national symbol is the northern bald ibis.","Damascus in Syria sits at an oasis fed by the Barada River and is thought to be one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities."],
  lb: ["Lebanon's name comes from the Semitic root lbn, meaning 'white', probably after its snow-capped mountains. Its national symbol is the cedar tree.","Beirut in Lebanon gets its name from be'erot, meaning 'the wells', which were once the only source of water in the region.","Lebanon is the smallest country in continental Asia."],
  il: ["Israel's Malham Cave, inside Mount Sodom, is the world's longest salt cave at 10 km. The hill itself is about 80% salt.","Israel's lowest point is the Dead Sea, whose shore lies 431 m below sea level and whose water is 9.6 times saltier than the ocean.","The music of Israel's anthem, 'Hatikvah' (The Hope), is thought to be based on a Romanian folk song, 'The Ox-Driven Cart'."],
  ps: ["Palestine's West Bank reaches down to the Dead Sea, whose shore lies 431 m below sea level and whose water is 9.6 times saltier than the ocean.","The highest point in Palestine's West Bank is Khallat al Batrakh, at 1,020 m above sea level.","The area of Palestine known as the West Bank has been inhabited since at least the 15th century B.C."],
  jo: ["Jordan's capital Amman began as Rabbath Ammon, 'The Capital of the Ammonites', a name later shortened to Ammon and then Amman.","The Dead Sea on Jordan's western border is the lowest point in Asia and 9.6 times saltier than the ocean.","Jordan is almost landlocked, with just a 26 km coastline and a single port, Aqaba."],
  iq: ["Iraq's name probably comes from Uruk, the ancient Sumerian and Babylonian city on the Euphrates River.","In Iraq, the Tigris and Euphrates rivers join to form a single waterway, the Shatt al Arab.","Iraq's highest peak, Cheekha Dar at 3,611 m, has a Kurdish name meaning 'Black Tent'."],
  sa: ["Saudi Arabia is the largest country in the world without a river.","Riyadh in Saudi Arabia takes its name from the Arabic word for 'gardens', as the city was built around a small oasis.","The 'Arabia' in Saudi Arabia goes back at least to the ancient Romans, who called the peninsula 'Arabia Felix' (Arabia the Fortunate)."],
  ae: ["Abu Dhabi in the United Arab Emirates means 'father of Dhabi', a personal name that comes from zab, the word for 'gazelle'.","The national symbol of the United Arab Emirates is the golden falcon.","The composer of the United Arab Emirates' anthem, Mohamad Abdel Wahab, also composed the music for Tunisia's and Libya's anthems."],
  qa: ["Doha in Qatar comes from the Arabic ad-dawha, 'the big tree', probably after a large tree at the original fishing village.","Qatar's national symbol is a white serrated band with nine white points on top of a maroon field.","Qatar's highest point, Tuwayyir al Hamir, is just 103 m above sea level."],
  bh: ["Bahrain means 'the two seas' in Arabic, after the waters on each side of the archipelago.","Manama in Bahrain comes from the Arabic al-manama, meaning 'place of rest' or 'place of dreams'.","Bahrain's national symbol is a white serrated band with five white points on top of a red field."],
  kw: ["Kuwait's name comes from the Arabic al-kuwayt, a diminutive of the Hindustani word kut, meaning a fortress-like house.","Kuwait's national symbol is the golden falcon.","Kuwait's highest point, at 300 m, is identified simply as a spot 3.6 km west of the Al-Salmi Border Post."],
  om: ["Muscat in Oman is said to mean 'hidden', after the range of hills that isolate the port city from the rest of the country.","Oman's anthem was first performed in 1932 by the band of the HMS Hawkins, whose bandmaster arranged it, as a salute to the Sultan.","Oman's name may be at least 2,000 years old: Pliny the Elder mentioned an 'Omana' in the 1st century A.D."],
  ye: ["Yemen's name may come from the Arabic al-yamin, meaning 'the right', a reference to its position in relation to Mecca.","Sana'a in Yemen is reputed to mean 'fortified place' in an ancient language.","Yemen sits on Bab el Mandeb, the strait linking the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden and one of the world's most active shipping lanes."],
  ir: ["Iran's former name, Persia, was originally 'Pars', from the Old Persian parsi, meaning 'pure'.","Iran's national symbol is the lion.","Iran ranges from the peak of Kuh-e Damavand at 5,625 m down to the Caspian Sea shore, 28 m below sea level."],
  ge: ["The world's four deepest caves are all in Georgia, including Veryovkina Cave, which reaches a depth of 2,212 m.","Tbilisi in Georgia comes from the Georgian word tbili, meaning 'warm', after the area's hot sulfur springs.","Georgia's national anthem, 'Tavisupleba' (Liberty), is based on music from the operas 'Abesalom da Eteri' and 'Daisi'."],
  am: ["By tradition, Armenia's local name, Hayastan, comes from Hayk, the legendary patriarch of the Armenians and great-great-grandson of Noah.","Armenia's capital Yerevan may take its name from the ancient fortress of Erebuni, built on the same site in 782 B.C.","Lake Sevan in Armenia, at 1,360 sq km, is the largest lake in the Lesser Caucasus Mountains."],
  az: ["Azerbaijan's name can be translated as 'Fire Keeper' or 'The Land of Fire', and its national symbol is flames of fire.","Baku in Azerbaijan may come from the Old Persian badkuba, meaning 'windward', after its windy spot on the shore of the Caspian Sea.","Azerbaijan is landlocked, yet its lowest point is on the Caspian Sea, a salt lake, at 28 m below sea level."],
  kz: ["Kazakhstan is the world's largest landlocked country and one of only two landlocked countries that extend into two continents.","Astana, the capital of Kazakhstan, simply means 'capital city' in Kazakh.","Kazakhstan's Pik Khan-Tengri, at 7,010 m, is the northernmost 7,000 m peak in the world."],
  uz: ["Uzbekistan is one of only two doubly landlocked countries in the world. The other is Liechtenstein.","Tashkent in Uzbekistan comes from the words tash (stone) and kent (town), so its name means 'stone town'.","Uzbekistan's national symbol is the khumo, a mythical bird."],
  tm: ["Ashgabat in Turkmenistan comes from the Turkmen words ushq, meaning 'love', and abad, meaning 'inhabited place' or 'town'.","The great Garagum (Kara-Kum) desert covers over 80% of Turkmenistan.","Turkmenistan's national symbol is the Akhal-Teke horse."],
  kg: ["Kyrgyzstan's Lake Issyk-Kul is surrounded by snow-capped mountains, yet it never freezes.","Kyrgyzstan is entirely mountainous: 94% of the country is 1,000 m above sea level, dominated by the Tien Shan range.","By tradition, Kyrgyzstan's name combines Turkic words for 'forty' and 'hundred', from a tale about two tribes and the number of their tents."],
  tj: ["Dushanbe in Tajikistan means 'Monday' in Persian, after a large bazaar that was held there on Mondays.","Tajikistan's highest peak, at 7,495 m, was the tallest mountain in the former USSR.","The Marco Polo sheep is one of Tajikistan's national symbols."],
  in: ["India's anthem, 'Jana-Gana-Mana', was written by Nobel laureate Rabindranath Tagore, who also wrote Bangladesh's national anthem.","India's official national symbol, the Lion Capital of Ashoka, shows four Asiatic lions standing back-to-back. The Bengal tiger and lotus are traditional ones.","Kanchenjunga, the third tallest mountain in the world at 8,586 m, lies on India's border with Nepal."],
  pk: ["Pakistan literally means 'Land of the Pure', from pak, meaning 'pure' in Persian or Pashto, and the suffix -stan, meaning 'country'.","Pakistan's highest point is K2, also called Mt. Godwin-Austen, at 8,611 m.","Jasmine is one of Pakistan's national symbols, alongside a five-pointed star between the horns of a crescent moon."],
  np: ["Nepal contains eight of the world's 10 highest peaks, including Mount Everest, the highest point on earth above sea level at 8,849 m.","Kathmandu in Nepal comes from kath (wooden) and mandu (temple), after local temples that are often still built from wood.","Nepal's national symbol is the rhododendron blossom, and its national anthem is called 'Hundreds of Flowers'."],
  bd: ["Bangladesh's name combines the Bengali words Bangla (Bengali) and desh (country).","Bangladesh's anthem, 'My Golden Bengal', was written by Nobel laureate Rabindranath Tagore, who also wrote India's national anthem.","Bangladesh's national symbols are the Bengal tiger and the water lily."],
  lk: ["Sri Lanka's name comes from the Sanskrit shri (happiness or holiness) and lanka (island). Its old name, Ceylon, came from simha, 'lion'.","Geological evidence suggests that Adam's Bridge, a 50 km chain of limestone shoals, once connected Sri Lanka and India.","Colombo in Sri Lanka may be named for local mango trees, from the Sinhalese words kola (leaves) and amba (mango)."],
  bt: ["Bhutan's local name, Druk Yul, means 'Land of the Dragon', and its national symbol is the mythical thunder dragon (druk).","By tradition, Thimphu in Bhutan is named after a local deity who dissolved before a traveler's eyes and became part of the rock the city stands on.","Bhutan's name may come from Sanskrit words meaning 'end of Tibet', as it lies at Tibet's southernmost end."],
  mv: ["The highest point in the Maldives is just 5 m above sea level, on the 8th tee of a golf course on Villingi Island.","The Maldives' name may come from the Sanskrit maladvipa, 'garland of islands'. It has 1,190 coral islands grouped into 26 atolls.","The national symbols of the Maldives are the coconut palm and the yellowfin tuna."],
  af: ["Afghanistan literally means 'Land of the Afghans', since the suffix -stan means 'place of' or 'country'.","Afghanistan's highest peaks are in the Wakhan Corridor, topped by Noshak at 7,492 m.","Afghanistan's national symbol is the lion."],
  cn: ["China has the world's largest sinkhole, Xiaoxhai Tiankeng in Chongqing, which is 660 m deep.","China's own name, Zhongguo, translates as 'Central Nation' or 'Middle Country'.","China's national symbols are the dragon and the giant panda."],
  jp: ["Japan's anthem, 'Kimigayo', has the oldest anthem lyrics in the world, dating to the 10th century or earlier.","Japan's capital Tokyo was once called Edo, meaning 'estuary'. In 1868 it became Tokyo, 'eastern capital', in contrast to Kyoto to the west.","A 2023 survey detected 100,000 islands and islets in Japan, but only 14,125 were officially counted."],
  kr: ["South Korea's national symbols include the Rose of Sharon, the Siberian tiger and the taegeuk, a yin-yang symbol.","About 3,000 mostly small and uninhabited islands lie off South Korea's western and southern coasts.","Seoul in South Korea gets its name from the Korean word meaning 'capital city'."],
  kp: ["North Korea's own name, Choson, means 'Land of the Morning Calm'.","Pyongyang in North Korea translates as 'flat land' in Korean.","One of North Korea's national symbols is the chollima, a winged horse."],
  mn: ["Mongolia's name comes from the Mongol root word mengu or mongu, meaning 'brave' or 'unconquered'.","Ulaanbaatar in Mongolia means 'red hero' in Mongolian, honoring national hero Damdin Sukhbaatar.","Mongolia's national symbol is the Soyombo character, from the Soyombo writing system."],
  tw: ["Taiwan's name may come from the Chinese words tai (terrace) and wan (bay). In 1590 the Portuguese named it Formosa, meaning 'beautiful'.","Taiwan's highest point is Yu Shan, at 3,952 m.","About 16 indigenous languages are spoken in Taiwan, alongside Mandarin, Min Nan and Hakka dialects."],
  th: ["Bangkok in Thailand has a full ceremonial name that holds the world record for longest place name. It begins 'City of angels, great city of immortals'.","Thailand's national symbols are the elephant and the garuda, a mythical half-man, half-bird figure.","The 'Thai' in Thailand comes from the Thai word tha, meaning 'to be free'. Its old name, Siam, comes from the Sanskrit syama, 'dark'."],
  vn: ["Vietnam's Son Doong is the world's largest cave, so massive it contains its own jungle, underground river and weather, with clouds forming inside.","Vietnam stretches 1,650 km from north to south but is only 50 km across at its narrowest point.","Hanoi in Vietnam means 'inside the river', as it sits in a bend of the Red River."],
  kh: ["Cambodia's national symbols include Angkor Wat temple and the kouprey, a wild ox.","Phnom Penh in Cambodia means 'mountain of plenty', from the Cambodian words phnom (mountain or hill) and penh (full).","Cambodia's Tonle Sap, Southeast Asia's largest freshwater lake, ranges from 2,700 to 16,000 sq km."],
  la: ["Vientiane, the capital of Laos, is called Viangchan in Laotian, meaning 'city of sandalwood'. Its standard spelling reflects French influence.","The elephant is the national symbol of Laos.","Laos is mostly mountainous and thickly forested, and the Mekong River forms a large part of its western border with Thailand."],
  mm: ["Myanmar's name comes from myanma, meaning 'the strong', the Burman people's name for themselves.","Naypyidaw (Nay Pyi Taw) in Myanmar translates as 'abode of kings'.","Myanmar's national symbol is the chinthe, a mythical lion."],
  my: ["Kuala Lumpur in Malaysia means 'muddy river junction', as it sits where the Kelang and Gombak rivers meet.","Malaysia's name was devised in the early 19th century by British geographers, who added -sia to the name of the Malay people.","Malaysia's national symbols are the tiger and the hibiscus."],
  sg: ["Singapore means 'lion city' in Sanskrit, yet lions aren't native: folklore says a 7th-century Indian prince mistook the first animal he saw for one.","Singapore's national symbols include the merlion, a mythical half-lion, half-fish creature, and the orchid.","Singapore is made up of about 60 islands, and land reclamation has removed many former islands and created new ones."],
  id: ["Indonesia is the world's largest country made up solely of islands: 13,466 of them, of which 922 are permanently inhabited.","Indonesia's Danau Toba is the largest volcanic lake in the world, sitting in the caldera of a super volcano.","Indonesia's name is an 18th-century blend of two Greek words, Indos (India) and nesoi (islands), meaning 'Indian islands'."],
  ph: ["The Philippines was named in honor of King Philip II of Spain by Spanish explorer Ruy Lopez de Villalobos, who visited the islands in 1543.","In 2016 the mapping authority of the Philippines reported hundreds of newly discovered islands, raising the count from 7,107 to 7,641.","Manila in the Philippines comes from the Tagalog may ('there is') and nila, a local shrub in the indigo family."],
  bn: ["Brunei's national symbol is the royal parasol.","Brunei is split into two parts by Malaysia. Its eastern part, the Temburong district, is an exclave.","Bandar Seri Begawan in Brunei combines bandar, Malay for 'city' or 'port', with Seri Begawan, roughly meaning 'honored lord'."],
  tl: ["Timor-Leste literally means 'Eastern-East': timur is Indonesian and Malay for 'east', and leste is Portuguese for 'east'.","Timor-Leste is the only Asian country located completely in the Southern Hemisphere.","Timor-Leste's national symbol is a mountain, Mount Ramelau."],
  eg: ["Egypt's English name comes from ancient Greek words that may mean 'land of the vulture', while its Arabic name, Misr, traces back to an Assyrian word for 'fort'.","Egypt's capital Cairo may take its name from 'al-qahir', an Arabic name for Mars, the planet rising on the day building began in 969 A.D.","Egypt's Sinai Peninsula is the only land bridge between Africa and the rest of the Eastern Hemisphere."],
  ma: ["Morocco's English name comes from Marrakesh, a former capital, while its Arabic name, Al Maghrib, means 'The West'.","Morocco's capital Rabat gets its name from 'ribat', Arabic for a fortified monastery, after a 12th-century fort built on the site.","Morocco is the only African nation with both an Atlantic and a Mediterranean coastline."],
  dz: ["Algeria is the largest country in Africa, about 80% of it desert, and the fennec fox is one of its national symbols.","Algeria is named after Algiers, from the Arabic for 'the islands': four islands off the coast that were joined to the mainland in 1525.","Caves and canyons in Algeria's Tassili n'Ajjer hold prehistoric rock paintings of elephants and giraffes, from a time when the region was green."],
  tn: ["Tunisia is named after its capital, Tunis, a name sometimes linked to the ancient Phoenician goddess Tanith.","The composer of Tunisia's national anthem, Mohamad Abdel Wahab, also wrote the music for the anthem of the United Arab Emirates.","Tunisia's lowest point, Shatt al Gharsah, lies 17 m below sea level."],
  ly: ["Libya's name probably comes from the Libu, an ancient tribe; the Greeks and Romans used it for the whole North African coast west of Egypt.","Libya's capital Tripoli gets its name from the Greek words for 'three cities'.","In the middle of Libya's Sahara sits the volcano Waw an Namus, whose crater is an oasis with small lakes; its name means 'oasis of mosquitoes'."],
  sd: ["Sudan's capital Khartoum means 'end of the elephant's trunk', describing the narrow strip of land where the Blue and White Niles meet.","Sudan's national symbol is the secretary bird.","Sudan, long known as Nubia, was home to the Kingdom of Kerma from about 2500 to 1500 B.C."],
  sn: ["Senegal's capital Dakar takes its name from the Wolof word 'n'dakar', meaning 'tamarind tree'.","Senegal is the westernmost country on the African continent.","Senegal's anthem is called 'Pluck Your Koras, Strike the Balafons', after a harp-like instrument and a type of xylophone."],
  mr: ["Mauritania is named after the ancient kingdom of Mauretania, whose name comes from the Mauri (Moors) of northwest Africa.","Mauritania's capital Nouakchott may mean 'place of the winds', though other theories include 'a place with pasture' and even 'without ears'.","Mauritania is considered part of both North Africa's Maghreb region and West Africa's Sahel region."],
  gm: ["Gambia is the smallest country on the African mainland and is almost an enclave of Senegal.","Gambia's capital Banjul got its name from a mix-up: locals thought they were asked what they were doing and replied 'bangjulo', or 'rope making'.","Gambia's highest point is an unnamed spot just 63 m above sea level."],
  gw: ["Guinea-Bissau adds 'Bissau', from the local Bijuga people, to its name to tell it apart from neighboring Guinea.","The music of Guinea-Bissau's national anthem is by Chinese composer Xiao He, heard by a delegation that visited China in 1963.","Guinea-Bissau is swampy along its western coast and low-lying inland; its highest point, Dongol Ronde, is just 277 m."],
  gn: ["Guinea's capital Conakry comes from the Susu word 'konakri', meaning 'over the water', because the city sits on a peninsula.","The Niger River, 4,200 km long, has its source in Guinea's highlands.","Guinea's national symbol is the elephant."],
  sl: ["Sierra Leone means 'Lion Mountains'; one early explorer linked the name to the rumbling of thunder over the mountains.","Rainfall on Sierra Leone's coast can reach 495 cm a year, making it one of the wettest places along coastal western Africa.","Sierra Leone's capital Freetown was named in 1781 as a haven for free-born and freed African Americans."],
  lr: ["Liberia's name comes from the Latin word 'liber', meaning 'free'.","Liberia's capital Monrovia is named after James Monroe, the fifth president of the United States.","The man who wrote the words of Liberia's national anthem, 'All Hail, Liberia, Hail!', later became the country's third president."],
  cv: ["Cape Verde means 'Green Cape', named after a peninsula on the coast of Senegal, the westernmost point of Africa.","Cape Verde's capital Praia was once called Villa de Praia, Portuguese for 'Village of the Beach'.","Cape Verde's highest point is Mt. Fogo, a 2,829 m volcano on Fogo Island."],
  ng: ["Nigeria is named for the Niger River, whose name probably comes from a Tuareg phrase meaning 'big rivers'.","Nigeria's capital Abuja is a newly built city that replaced Lagos in 1991; its name comes from a nearby town named after Abu Ja, 'Abu the Red'.","Nigeria shares Lake Chad, whose area changes with the seasons, from about 10,360 to 25,900 sq km."],
  gh: ["Ghana's Lake Volta is the world's largest artificial lake by surface area, covering 8,482 sq km.","Ghana's capital Accra takes its name from the Akan word 'nkran', meaning 'ant'.","Ghana was once called the Gold Coast, after the gold Portuguese explorers found there in the late 15th century."],
  ci: ["Ivory Coast's name reflects the ivory trade of the 15th to 17th centuries; since 1986 it has asked to be called by its French name.","Ivory Coast's city of Abidjan may be named after a mix-up: when asked the village's name, women replied 'I return from cutting leaves'.","Ivory Coast has 60 native dialects alongside French, and Dioula is the most widely spoken."],
  tg: ["Togo's name comes from the town of Togodo on Lake Togo, from the Ewe words 'to' (water) and 'go' (shore).","Togo's capital Lomé takes its name from a local word meaning 'little market'.","Togo has a coastline of just 56 km, yet it stretches through six distinct geographic regions."],
  bj: ["Benin's capital Porto-Novo is Portuguese for 'new port'.","Benin was once called Dahomey, after an earlier kingdom in the area called Dan Homé.","Benin's national symbol is the leopard."],
  bf: ["Burkina Faso means 'Land of the Worthy Men' in the Dyula language.","Burkina Faso's national anthem was written by a former president of the country who was an avid guitar player.","Burkina Faso is crossed by three main rivers named by color: the Black, Red and White Voltas."],
  ml: ["Mali's national symbol is a building: the Great Mosque of Djenne.","Mali is named after the Mali Empire, which at its 14th-century peak was the largest and wealthiest empire in West Africa.","Mali's capital Bamako has a name from the Bambara language that may refer to a crocodile."],
  ne: ["Niger is one of the hottest countries in the world, and its northern four-fifths is desert.","Niger's national symbol is the zebu.","Niger is named for the Niger River, whose name probably comes from a Tuareg phrase meaning 'big rivers'."],
  cm: ["Cameroon's name comes from Rio dos Camaroes, 'River of Prawns', given by Portuguese explorers after the abundant shrimp they found.","Cameroon is sometimes called the 'hinge of Africa' because of its central location on the continent.","Cameroon's highest point, Fako on Mount Cameroon, is an active volcano rising to 4,045 m."],
  td: ["Chad is named for Lake Chad, a remnant of ancient Lake Mega-Chad, which once covered about 400,000 sq km, roughly the size of the Caspian Sea.","Chad's Lakes of Ounianga, 18 linked freshwater and salty lakes, are the last remnant of the 'Green Sahara'.","Chad has two national symbols: the goat in the north and the lion in the south."],
  cf: ["Central African Republic lies almost at the precise center of Africa.","Central African Republic's capital Bangui means 'rapids', because the city sits above the first great rapid on the Ubangi River.","The word 'Africa' in Central African Republic's name comes from the Romans' 'Africa terra', 'Land of the Afri', an area in today's Tunisia."],
  gq: ["Despite its name, no part of the equator passes through Equatorial Guinea; its mainland lies just north of it.","Equatorial Guinea's city of Malabo was first named Port Clarence, then Santa Isabel, before being named after King Malabo, the last Bubi king, in 1973.","Equatorial Guinea's official languages include Spanish, Portuguese and French."],
  ga: ["Gabon's name comes from the Portuguese word 'gabão', meaning 'cloak', possibly describing the shape of the Komo River estuary.","Gabon's capital Libreville means 'free town' in French.","Gabon's national symbol is the black panther."],
  cg: ["Republic of the Congo is named for the Congo River, which forms much of its eastern border; the name comes from the Bantu kingdom of Kongo.","Republic of the Congo's capital Brazzaville is named after explorer Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, who founded the town in 1883.","Republic of the Congo has two national symbols: the lion and the elephant."],
  cd: ["DR Congo's Congo River is considered the deepest river in the world, with its deepest point estimated at 220 to 250 m.","DR Congo is the second-largest country in Africa, yet its only outlet to the Atlantic is a coastline just 37 km long.","DR Congo's highest point is Pic Marguerite on Mount Stanley, at 5,110 m."],
  st: ["São Tomé and Príncipe's main island is named after Saint Thomas, because the Portuguese found it on his feast day, 21 December.","Príncipe, in São Tomé and Príncipe, is short for 'Ilha do Principe', the Isle of the Prince, after Prince Alphonso of Portugal.","São Tomé and Príncipe is Africa's second-smallest country, and its two main islands are part of a chain of extinct volcanoes."],
  et: ["Ethiopia's capital Addis Ababa means 'new flower' in Amharic; Empress Taitu gave the city its name in 1887.","The Blue Nile, the Nile's chief headstream by water volume, rises in Lake Tana in northwest Ethiopia.","The oldest hominid yet found comes from Ethiopia."],
  ke: ["Kenya is named for Mount Kenya, maybe from a Kikuyu phrase for 'white mountain'; Africa's second-highest peak even has glaciers.","Kenya's capital Nairobi takes its name from a Maasai expression meaning 'cool waters'.","Kenya shares Lake Victoria, the world's largest tropical lake, with Tanzania and Uganda."],
  tz: ["Tanzania's name combines the first letters of Tanganyika and Zanzibar, which merged to form the country in 1964.","Tanzania's Kilimanjaro, at 5,895 m, is the highest point in Africa and one of only three mountain ranges on the continent with glaciers.","Tanzania's national anthem shares its melody with Zambia's anthem and is also part of South Africa's anthem."],
  ug: ["Uganda's capital Kampala is said to be named after the impala, an African antelope.","Uganda's anthem, 'O Uganda, Land of Beauty!', is one of the shortest national anthems in the world.","Uganda's national symbol is the grey crowned crane."],
  rw: ["Rwanda's national symbol is a traditional woven basket with a peaked lid.","Rwanda's capital Kigali is named after nearby Mount Kigali, from the Rwandan word 'gali', meaning 'broad'.","Rwanda is home to a source of the Nile, which flows 6,650 km to its mouth in Egypt."],
  bi: ["Burundi's name uses Bantu prefixes: 'ba-' names the people (Barundi) and 'bu-' names the country (Burundi).","Burundi sits on the Nile-Congo watershed, and its Kagera River is the most remote headstream of the White Nile.","Even Burundi's lowest point, on Lake Tanganyika, is 772 m above sea level."],
  so: ["Somalia means 'Land of the Somali'; one idea is that 'Somali' comes from 'soo mal', 'go and milk', about offering guests milk.","Somalia's capital Mogadishu probably takes its name from the Arabic word 'mukaddas', meaning 'holy'.","Somalia sits on the Horn of Africa and has a coastline of 3,025 km."],
  dj: ["Djibouti's Lake Assal, 155 m below sea level, is the lowest point in Africa and the saltiest lake in the world.","Djibouti's name is said to come from the Afar word 'gabouri', meaning 'plate', after a palm-fiber plate used in ceremonies.","Djibouti sits near the world's busiest shipping lanes."],
  er: ["Eritrea's name comes from the ancient Greek 'Erythra Thalassa', meaning 'Red Sea', the sea along its coast.","Eritrea's capital Asmara means 'flowery wood' in the Tigrinya language.","Eritrea's national symbol is the camel."],
  ss: ["South Sudan, independent since 2011, is the world's newest country.","South Sudan's Sudd, a vast swamp formed by the White Nile, can cover some 15% of the country in the rainy season.","South Sudan's anthem, 'South Sudan Oyee!' ('South Sudan, Hooray!'), was chosen in a national contest."],
  za: ["South Africa completely surrounds Lesotho and almost completely surrounds Eswatini.","Africa's southernmost point is South Africa's Cape Agulhas, where the Atlantic and Indian Oceans meet, not the Cape of Good Hope.","South Africa's national anthem mixes five languages: Xhosa, Zulu, Sesotho, Afrikaans and English."],
  na: ["Namibia is named for the Namib Desert, considered the world's oldest; 'namib' means 'an area where there is nothing' in Nama.","Namibia's capital Windhoek gets its name from an Afrikaans word meaning 'windy corner'.","Namibia's national symbol is the oryx, a type of antelope."],
  bw: ["Botswana's border with Zambia is only 0.15 km (150 m) long.","The Kalahari Desert covers about 70% of Botswana, while the Okavango Delta is one of the largest inland deltas in the world.","Botswana's capital Gaborone is named after a Tlokwa chief whose name means 'it is not unbecoming'."],
  zw: ["Zimbabwe's name comes from a Bantu phrase meaning 'houses of stones', after Great Zimbabwe, the stone-built capital of an old kingdom.","In full flood, Victoria Falls on Zimbabwe's Zambezi River forms the world's largest curtain of falling water.","Zimbabwe's capital Harare is named after a Shona chieftain whose name meant 'he who does not sleep'."],
  mz: ["Mozambique's capital Maputo is named after the Maputo River, said to be named after the son of an 18th-century local chief.","Both the Zambezi and the Limpopo rivers reach the sea in Mozambique.","Mozambique's coastline on the Indian Ocean stretches 2,470 km."],
  zm: ["Zambia is named after the Zambezi River, which forms its southern border with Zimbabwe.","Lake Kariba, on the border of Zambia and Zimbabwe, is the world's largest reservoir by volume, holding 180 cu km of water.","Both the Congo and Zambezi rivers have their sources in Zambia."],
  ao: ["Angola's name comes from 'N'gola', the title held by the kings of the Ndongo.","Angola's national symbol is the giant black sable antelope, the 'Palanca negra gigante'.","Angola's province of Cabinda is an exclave, separated from the rest of the country by DR Congo."],
  mw: ["Malawi is named for the Maravi people; the word 'maravi' means 'flames'.","Malawi's Lake Nyasa is about 580 km long and has more fish species than any other lake on earth.","Malawi's highest point is Sapitwa on Mount Mlanje, at 3,002 m."],
  ls: ["Lesotho is completely surrounded by South Africa, and more than 80% of the country lies over 1,800 m above sea level.","Lesotho's national symbol is the mokorotio, the traditional Basotho hat.","The music of Lesotho's national anthem comes from an 1823 Swiss songbook."],
  sz: ["Eswatini is named after Mswati II, a 19th-century king who expanded and unified Swati territory.","Eswatini is landlocked and almost completely surrounded by South Africa.","Eswatini's capital Mbabane may be named after a river whose name comes from 'lubabe', a type of shrub."],
  mg: ["Madagascar's name traces back to Marco Polo, who confused the island with the Somali port of Mogadishu.","Madagascar's capital Antananarivo means 'City of the Thousand', after 1,000 soldiers stationed at its original fortress.","About 90% of the plants and animals on Madagascar, the world's fourth-largest island, are found nowhere else."],
  mu: ["Mauritius is named after Prince Maurice van Nassau, governor of the Dutch Republic, in 1598.","Mauritius's national symbols include the dodo, a large flightless bird related to pigeons that once lived on the island.","The main island of Mauritius is volcanic and almost entirely surrounded by coral reefs."],
  sc: ["Seychelles is the smallest African country, made up of 155 islands: 42 granitic and 113 coralline.","Seychelles' national symbol is the coco de mer, or 'sea coconut'.","Seychelles is named after Jean Moreau de Séchelles, a French finance minister; the British changed the spelling in 1815."],
  km: ["Comoros gets its name from the Arabic 'al qamar', meaning 'the moon'.","Comoros' capital Moroni means 'at the place of fire', because it lies below the active volcano Mt. Karthala.","Comoros is the only Arab League country that lies entirely in the Southern Hemisphere."],
  au: ["Australia's name comes from the Latin australis, meaning 'southern'; the landmass was long called Terra Australis, the Southern Land.","Perth, in Australia, has a sea breeze nicknamed the 'Fremantle Doctor', one of the most consistent winds in the world.","Australia's national symbols include the kangaroo, the emu and the golden wattle tree."],
  nz: ["New Zealand's Maori name, Aotearoa, is in legend the name of the canoe that Kupe, the first Polynesian in New Zealand, sailed there.","New Zealand's national symbols include the kiwi bird, the silver fern and the Southern Cross constellation.","New Zealand's capital, Wellington, is the southernmost national capital in the world."],
  pg: ["Papua New Guinea's national symbol is the bird of paradise.","When gold prospectors reached the highlands of Papua New Guinea in the 1920s, they found about 1 million people living in isolated communities.","Papua New Guinea's capital, Port Moresby, was named in 1873 by Captain John Moresby in honor of his father, Admiral Sir Fairfax Moresby."],
  fj: ["Fijians called their home Viti, but neighboring Tongans called it Fisi; an anglicized spelling of the Tongan version became Fiji.","Fiji is made up of 332 islands, about 110 of them inhabited, plus more than 500 islets.","Fiji's capital, Suva, means 'little hill' in Fijian and may refer to a mound where a temple once stood."],
  sb: ["The Solomon Islands were named in 1568 after the wealthy biblical King Solomon, in the mistaken belief that the islands held great riches.","Lake Tegano on Rennell Island, Solomon Islands, was once a lagoon of the atoll and is now the largest lake in the insular Pacific.","The name of the Solomon Islands' capital, Honiara, comes from nagho ni ara, meaning 'place of the east wind' or 'facing the trade winds'."],
  vu: ["Vanuatu means 'Our land forever'; Captain James Cook had called it New Hebrides because it reminded him of the Hebrides islands off Scotland.","Vanuatu's national anthem is called 'Yumi, Yumi, Yumi' (We, We, We) and is written in the native Bislama.","Vanuatu is a Y-shaped chain of four main islands and 80 smaller ones, with active volcanoes, including several underwater."],
  ws: ["Samoa's name may mean 'place of the moa bird' from Polynesian mythology, or it may come from a local chieftain's name.","Samoa sits in an almost central position within Polynesia.","Samoa's national symbol is the Southern Cross constellation, shown as five five-pointed stars."],
  to: ["Tonga's name is said to mean 'island'; Captain James Cook called it the Friendly Islands in 1773 because of the welcome he received.","Tonga's capital, Nuku'alofa, is said to combine the local words nuku ('residence or abode') and alofa ('love').","Tonga's King George Tupou I, crowned in 1845, founded the only Polynesian monarchy that still exists today."],
  ki: ["Kiribati is the only country in the world that falls into all four hemispheres: northern, southern, eastern and western.","Kiribati is the local pronunciation of 'Gilbert', after explorer Thomas Gilbert, who mapped many of the islands in 1788.","Kiribati's capital, Tarawa, is said to come from the words te (the) and rawa (run), referring to a channel through a nearby reef."],
  tv: ["Tuvalu means 'eight standing together', but it has nine islands: Nui was left out of the grouping because its people spoke a different language.","Tuvalu's highest point is an unnamed spot just 5 m above sea level.","Tuvalu's capital, Funafuti, may get its name from futi, the Polynesian word for banana."],
  nr: ["Naoero, called Nauru until July 2026, may get its name from the local word 'anaoero', meaning 'I go to the beach'.","Naoero is the world's smallest island country and its smallest independent republic.","Ocean currents made landing on Naoero difficult, so it developed in isolation, and its language doesn't clearly resemble any other in the Pacific."],
  mh: ["Kwajalein atoll in the Marshall Islands surrounds the world's largest lagoon.","The early people of the Marshall Islands were skilled navigators who traveled between atolls using stick charts to map the islands.","The Marshall Islands' capital, Majuro, means 'two openings' or 'two eyes', after the two major passages through the atoll into its lagoon."],
  fm: ["Micronesia's name is built from two Greek words, mikros (small) and nesoi (islands), after its many small islands.","Micronesia is made up of four major island groups totaling 607 islands.","On Pohnpei, in Micronesia, people began building the artificial islets of Nan Madol in the 800s A.D."],
  pw: ["Palau's capital, Ngerulmud, means 'place of fermented angelfish': women traditionally gathered there to offer fermented angelfish to the gods.","Long ago, Palau developed a highly organized matrilineal society where high-ranking women picked the chiefs.","Palau has more than 300 islands in six groups, including the world-famous Rock Islands."],
};
